      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Thanks!\n\u003e \n\u003e I don\u0027t yet believe this is sufficient though.\n\u003e \n\u003e From what you say on the bug, it sounds like the motivation is that applications might want to blend themselves. Given that libwebp always composites on top of fully transparent black,\n\nlibwebp doesn\u0027t do the compositing, it will just decode the individual frames\npreserving the alpha. You can see the individual frames using `webpmux -get\nframe 0/1 test.webp frame_0/1.webp` and then use `dwebp` or a viewer to display them.\nAnimDecode will composite and I think it does ignore the background currently,\nbut the background written will be transparent (seen with `anim_dump`), so again\nit\u0027s up to the renderer to set a background. `vwebp` has `-usebgcolor` for\nthis.\n\n\u003e I think you\u0027re saying that applications would do something like:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Get the ANIM section\u0027s background color for that file form webp\n\u003e 2. Clear a drawing canvas\u0027s background to background color\n\u003e 3. Get frame from libwebp (which has transparent black in transparent areas)\n\u003e 4. Blent it on top of the canvas\n\u003e \n\u003e That would work.\n\u003e \n\u003e I think the intent of the pseudocode here is to produce the same output as these 4 steps, by commingling steps 2 and 4.\n\u003e \n\u003e But it has a different semantics. For the file I attached to the bug (which was written by libwebp):\n\u003e \n\u003e [...]\n\u003e \n\u003e The background color in ANIM is #ffffffff.\n\u003e \n\u003e There are two frames. Neither frame covers the entire canvas. Both frames set dispose to 0 (ie \"do not dispose, leave canvas as is\") and blend to 1 (\"do not blend; e, render the current frame on the canvas by overwriting the rectangle covered by the current frame.\").\n\u003e \n\u003e The frame data in the VP8Ls stores background pixels as #00000000.\n\u003e \n\u003e If we follow the steps in the pseudocode and \"Dispose to background color\", then we\u0027ll clear the pixels not covered by either of these frames to #ffffffff. That looks wrong when e.g. then saving out image data as png.\n\nThe clear of the full canvas to the background color is at the start of the\nloop. So in this case if you use the background color you clear to white at the\nstart, apply frame 1 (which has transparent areas) and then frame 2 to the\nunchanged canvas. If you have a clearer idea for the loop in the pseudocode,\nplease post it here or in the bug and we can discuss the difference in\nsemantics.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e I think a better way of spec\u0027ing this might be to describe the assembly algorithm to always use #00000000 as clear color for producing frame data,\n\nThat transparency needs to come from the alpha in the source image to go through to the background. Here you mean that the canvas should be viewed as fully transparent to begin with then an optional background can be applied?\n\n\u003e and saying that the frame data can be blended on top of ANIM background color when showing image data to the user and the displaying application does not have an opinion what else it wants to use as background color -- i.e. ANIM background color is a display hint, while frame decoding for pixel data is normative.\n\nThe doc does mention background color is a hint:\n\n```\n  * Viewer applications SHOULD treat the background color value as a hint and\n    are not required to use it.\n```",
